      "s": "Bloomberg Tech",
      "t": "Robinhood CEO Says New AI-Driven Agent Apps Are Safe",
      "d": "Robinhood Markets Chairman and CEO Vlad Tenev discusses the firm’s new AI-driven agent apps unveiled during a Houston summit. Speaking on \"Bloomberg The Close,\" Tenev outlines a vision of giving individual traders hedge fund-like tools, including 24/7 trading across asset classes, autonomous “agent loops” that can operate while users sleep, and access to specialized data sources such as satellite ",
      "u": "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2026-09-30/robinhood-ceo-says-new-ai-driven-agent-apps-are-safe-video",
      "m": "09. 30. PM 07:52",
      "ts": 1790797955000,
      "fullText": ""
    },
    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "BMW built the same car for gas and electric. The EV is $4,400 cheaper.",
      "d": "The new BMW 3 Series shows just how quickly EVs have caught up to fossil fuel vehicles on pricing.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/30/bmw-built-the-same-car-for-gas-and-electric-the-ev-is-4400-cheaper/",
      "m": "09. 30. PM 07:19",
      "ts": 1790795980000,
      "fullText": "For years, automotive analysts have been predicting that electric vehicles would become cheaper than their gas-powered equivalents. They figured it would happen sometime in the latter half of the 2020s. BloombergNEF , for example, expected it to happen in 2026. They weren’t far off. EV prices have dropped significantly in the last few years, but it has been hard to compare prices with fossil fuel vehicles because not every EV has a like-for-like gas-powered model. BMW has given us one. The 2027 BMW 3 Series comes in both gas and electric versions, and the EV is $4,400 cheaper . The gas-powered M350 xDrive will set you back $65,900, while the electric i3 50 xDrive will cost $61,500; neither price includes destination charges. Both are similarly powerful, similarly styled, and aimed at similar pocketbooks. The difference is the EV is 6.7% cheaper. Pedants will point out there’s a significantly cheaper gas-powered 3 Series trim, though it has no electric equivalent yet. The 2027 BMW 330 is priced at $49,900, and its upgraded xDrive trim costs $51,900. The two comparable vehicles are built on different platforms, with the EV i3 benefiting from BMW’s newest chassis and technology. The gas-powered 3 Series makes do with an overhauled version of an existing platform. For most people, the biggest difference will be the charging or refueling experience, but even there they’re more similar than some might expect. The range of the new gas 3 Series is likely similar to previous models, or about 450 miles per tank in all-wheel-drive guise. The electric i3 will travel 468 miles on a full charge, and BMW says the vehicle’s fast charging is speedy enough to replenish 208 miles of range in 10 minutes. The difference might be noticeable to extreme road warriors, but for most drivers, it’ll be academic. The generally accepted wisdom is that EVs have cost more up front, even if they’ve delivered a lower total cost of ownership . But now EVs are gaining the upper hand on purchase price,"
    },
    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "OpenAI&#8217;s Jev clone could help the frontier lab stop its swarming agents",
      "d": "OpenAI's \"Decisions API\" is a Jev clone that confirms the importance of fast, cheap intelligence.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/30/openais-jev-clone-could-help-the-frontier-lab-stop-its-swarming-agents/",
      "m": "09. 30. PM 07:00",
      "ts": 1790794857000,
      "fullText": "One of the more intriguing announcements at OpenAI&#8217;s Dev Day event on Tuesday came in an aside from CEO Sam Altman, who revealed the company&#8217;s new &#8220;Decisions API.&#8221; The API apparently provides similar functionality to Jev , a model released by TypeSafe AI earlier this month that&#8217;s explicitly designed for software automation. A kind of super-powered classifier built on an LLM, developers can give Jev a set of choices that it outputs as probabilities cheaply and at high speeds. OpenAI&#8217;s Decisions API seems to be the same sort of product. At the event, Altman described the API as a way to give the lab&#8217;s Luna model a predefined set of options to choose between, such as categories in which to classify an image or different agent behaviors. &#8220;By focusing the model on that choice, we can make it extremely fast while keeping capabilities like image understanding, broad language support, and safety protections,&#8221; Altman said. TypeSafe didn&#8217;t respond to TechCrunch&#8217;s questions about the new product, but CEO Diogo Almeida, a former OpenAI engineer who co-invented reinforcement learning, joked on X about the beginning of the clone wars. He added that OpenAI&#8217;s interest could be &#8220;a sign&#8230;that building in a System One compatible way is the future.&#8221; (&#8220;System One&#8221; is TypeSafe&#8217;s term of art for fast, intuitive thinking, versus &#8220;System 2,&#8221; which it applies to deliberate reasoning.) The subtext here is that LLMs as we know them aren&#8217;t the right solution for a lot of software because they are comparatively slow and expensive. Developers have been using Jev to augment LLMs and, in doing so, have found that they&#8217;re faster and cheaper. It&#8217;s not clear how similar Decisions API will be to Jev, since OpenAI released it as a limited preview and, thus far, TechCrunch hasn&#8217;t spotted developers running it through its paces. However, there is clearly interest ,"
    },
